
Problem Statement: Sentiment Analysis


An online shopping company wants to analyze customer reviews automatically to
understand customer satisfaction. Your task is to design a prompt that identifies the
sentiment expressed in a customer review.
Classify each review into one of three categories based on the customer's overall
sentiment.
Input: A customer review in natural language.
Output: A single word representing the sentiment.
Allowed Outputs:
• Positive
• Negative
• Neutral

Examples:
Input Review Expected Output
The product is excellent, and I love its quality. Positive
The delivery was late, and the product was damaged. Negative
The product arrived yesterday. Neutral
The customer support team was extremely helpful. Positive
The product is not worth the money. Negative
The package contained two items. Neutral
The phone is good, but the battery life is terrible. Negative

Constraint: The output must contain exactly one word: Positive, Negative, or Neutral.
Do not provide explanations or additional text.



prompt:


Create an AI-based sentiment analysis model for an online shopping company. The main purpose of this model is to read customer reviews, understand their opinions and automatically identify their overall satisfaction level.

Classify every customer review into one of the following three categories:

Positive – When the customer is happy, satisfied, or appreciates the product or service.
Negative – When the customer is unhappy, disappointed, or complains about the product or service.
Neutral – When the review is based on facts or information without showing a clear positive or negative feeling.
Rules and Constraints:

Read and understand the complete review before classifying it.
Focus on the customer's overall feeling rather than individual words.
If the review contains both positive and negative comments, classify it based on the overall sentiment.
Do not assume that a review is positive just because it contains words like "good" or "nice".
Treat complaints about damaged products, poor quality, late delivery, or bad customer service as Negative.
Treat reviews expressing satisfaction with product quality, delivery, or service as Positive.
If the review is unclear, too short, or does not express any specific opinion, classify it as Neutral.
Do not create or assume information that is not mentioned in the review.
Every review must receive only ONE category.
Use only the three allowed outputs: Positive, Negative, or Neutral.
Do not provide extra explanations, suggestions, or personal opinions.
Maintain consistent classification for all reviews.

Important: The final answer must always be one of the three allowed categories. The model should be simple, accurate, unbiased, and consistent when analyzing customer reviews.

